  "content": {
    "id": "p5-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "Suppose $a,b,c,d$ are four consecutive terms in an arithmetic progression. Given that $b\\ne c$ and $b+c\\ne0$, evaluate $\\dfrac{d^2-a^2}{c^2-b^2}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$3$.\n\nFactorise both differences of squares and use the equal consecutive differences.",
      "worked": "Let the common difference be $r$. Then $b-a=c-b=d-c=r$, with $r\\ne0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}d-a&=3r\\\\d+a&=(a+3r)+a\\\\d+a&=b+c.\\end{align*}$$\n\nFactorising gives\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{d^2-a^2}{c^2-b^2}&=\\frac{(d-a)(d+a)}{(c-b)(c+b)}\\\\\\frac{d^2-a^2}{c^2-b^2}&=\\frac{3r(b+c)}{r(b+c)}\\\\\\frac{d^2-a^2}{c^2-b^2}&=3.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 42
  },
